  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1059 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will shift directly overhead this weekend with \ntemperatures warming up once again. Highs will generally be in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across the area with lows in the mid to upper \n70s. Dew points will rise, but will not be extremely high given the \nongoing dry conditions. Even so, heat indices will marginally \nsupport the issuance of heat products at times, so this potential \nwill be monitored.\n\nWith the high overhead, no rain is expected to occur through \nearly next week. Winds will become southerly on Saturday, remaining \nthat way through the forecast period. Speeds will not be overly \nstrong, but a bit of gustiness is expected each afternoon. The hot, \ndry, and breezy conditions in combination with existing drought will \nsupport limited fire weather potential each day.\n\nBy the middle of next week, a pacific trough will nudge into the\nwest, weakening the southern Plains high along its western \nperiphery. Rain is still not likely, but a few isolated showers \nand storms cannot be ruled out for the middle to end of next week.\nIf these storms did develop, impacts would likely be limited to \nbrief locally heavy rain and gusty winds.",
